        "pull_request": {
          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/discord/ex_hash_ring/pulls/10",
          "merged_at": "2020-12-03T21:37:30Z"
        },
        "body": "This is a ~~minimal~~ maximal version of stable secondaries.\r\n\r\n# Work left to do\r\n\r\n- [x] Atomic remove_nodes for removing multiple nodes\r\n- [x] Remove or Update the non-ETS HashRing\r\n- [x] Refactor?\r\n- [x] Testing for GC behavior for multiple generations.\r\n\r\n# How it works\r\n\r\nRings can now be configured with a `depth` setting which controls how many generations back from the current generation it should retain.\r\n \r\nNew APIs expose the ability to query previous generations either in aggregate or separately.\r\n\r\n# API\r\n\r\nWhen resolving a target to a node with secondaries the `find_nodes/3` function is used.  This function works exactly as it does in master, new functions have been added to provide additional functionality.\r\n\r\n- `find_nodes/3` Finds the nodes responsible for a target by querying the ring generation's `current ring`\r\n- `find_historical_nodes/4` Finds the nodes responsible for a target by querying the specified generation.  Since the caller is unlikely to have the generation on hand this is expressed as number of generations to go back, so the current generation is 0, the immediately previous generation is 1, and so on.\r\n- `find_stable_nodes/4` Finds the nodes responsible for a target by querying and aggregating the results back to the generation offset provided.  This offset is the same as it is for find_historical_nodes, so 0 would only query the current generation, 1 would query and aggregate the current generation and the immediately previous generation, and so on.\r\n\r\n## Overrides no longer create generations\r\n\r\nThis is more of a question for @likeanocean I didn't see any reason that changing just the overrides would require creating a new generation and since the stability of the ring requires minimizing generation churn, I refactored the code slightly so that override changes wouldn't create new generations.  If my analysis is mistaken and this is an important feature of overrides, please let me know.\r\n\r\n## Utils.take_max/2\r\n\r\nThis function took some pains to construct the list forward (identical to Enum.take) for it to be reversed and then reversed again.  Since this isn't really a general purpose utility but a more custom job, I decided to build the list reversed to avoid an Enum.reverse/1 call.",
